# Chrome Browser Extension Development Rules

## Manifest Configuration
- Always use manifest_version 3 (MV3) for new extensions
- Include permissions only for APIs that are actually used
- Use host_permissions sparingly and with specific patterns
- Include action or page_action in manifest for extension icon/popup

## File Structure
- Keep manifest.json in the root directory
- Place service workers in /background or /service-workers
- Place content scripts in /content
- Place popups/sidepanels in /ui or /pages
- Keep assets in /images, /styles, /scripts directories

## Service Workers (Background Scripts)
- Service workers are the new background scripts for MV3
- They unload after 5 minutes of inactivity
- Use chrome.alarms for persistent timers, not setInterval
- Store persistent state in chrome.storage, not memory
- Handle chrome.runtime.onInstalled for initialization

## Content Scripts
- Inject only when necessary using matches/exclude_matches
- Use isolated worlds to prevent conflicts with page scripts
- Communicate with service workers via chrome.runtime.sendMessage
- Never assume DOM elements exist; check before access
- Avoid modifying global scope directly

## Messaging & Communication
- Use chrome.runtime.sendMessage for tab-to-service-worker
- Use chrome.tabs.sendMessage for tab-to-content-script
- Always handle response callbacks or use Promises
- Include error handling for message failures
- Use message ports for long-lived connections

## Storage
- Use chrome.storage.local for most data
- Use chrome.storage.sync for user preferences across devices
- Limit to 10MB per extension (sync storage: 100KB)
- Always use async methods (promises/callbacks)
- Handle storage quota limits gracefully

## Permissions & Security
- Request permissions only when needed
- Use activeTab permission instead of broad host permissions
- Never eval() or execute arbitrary code
- Sanitize all user input before DOM insertion
- Use Content Security Policy to prevent injection attacks

## APIs to Avoid (MV2 Only)
- chrome.extension.getBackgroundPage - use messaging instead
- chrome.extension.onRequest - use chrome.runtime instead
- Background pages - use service workers instead

## Testing
- Test with Chrome in Developer mode
- Test with multiple extensions loaded
- Verify permissions warnings are clear
- Test service worker reload behavior
- Check for memory leaks in long-running extensions

## Common APIs
- chrome.tabs.* for tab manipulation
- chrome.windows.* for window management
- chrome.storage.* for data persistence
- chrome.alarms.* for scheduling
- chrome.declarativeNetRequest.* for network filtering
- chrome.contextMenus.* for right-click menus
- chrome.notifications.* for user alerts
- chrome.runtime.* for extension management
